Dear everyone,<div>After confirming scalar invariance for my ordinal MG-CFA model, I was attempting to estimate latent factor scores, but got one negative residual variance that forbids me from doing so. My dataset contains n = ~31,000 and 14 groups, for model:</div><div>```</div><div><span style="color:rgb(42,60,66);font-size:0.9em">model &lt;- &#39;
y1 =~ x1 + x2 </span></div><div><span style="color:rgb(42,60,66);font-size:0.9em">                  y2 =~ x3 + x4
&#39;</span></div><div>```<br></div><div>wich I call with:</div><div><br></div><div><div>```</div><div>mgcfa_scalarfit &lt;- cfa(cfamodel, ordered = TRUE, data = df, group = &quot;countrynameyear&quot;, group.equal = c(&quot;loadings&quot;, &quot;thresholds&quot;, &quot;intercepts&quot;), <a href="http://std.lv" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://std.lv&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792414529998000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2qfrHdsCvQweA1azUjvGFi">std.lv</a> = TRUE)<br></div><div>```</div></div><div>For my eleventh group, labelled `New Zealand_2017`, residual variance of variable x1 came negative. I understand this is a Heywood case, and that I should compare the standard model with a model where the residual variance has been constrained to 0 (or between 0 and 1?), and check whether the difference in fit between the two models is significant. As per my understanding, lavWaldtest() would be a correct tool to check for this.<br></div><div><br></div><div>However, I am struggling to identify how to constrain the model for the residual variance of x1 to be 0.  I attempted:</div><div><div>```</div><div><span style="color:rgb(42,60,66);font-size:0.9em">constraint &lt;- &#39; </span>  ancestry ~~ c(NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, z11, NA, NA, NA) * ancestry<span style="color:rgb(42,60,66);font-size:0.9em"> &#39;</span></div><div><span style="color:rgb(42,60,66);font-size:0.9em">z11 == 0</span></div><div>```</div></div><div>and </div><div><div>```</div><div><span style="color:rgb(42,60,66);font-size:0.9em">constraint &lt;- &#39; </span>  ancestry ~~ c(NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, NA, 0, NA, NA, NA) * ancestry<span style="color:rgb(42,60,66);font-size:0.9em"> &#39;</span></div><div>```</div></div><div>but neither did this work to constrain the residual variance when inserting it directly within the code of the model, nor within the constraints = argument in lavWaldtest().</div><div>Therefore, does anybody have any particular idea what I am doing wrong or any suggestion on how to actually do it? That would be life saving (or near). </div><div>Thank you!</div><div><br></div>
